WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Strider, the free world relies on your success.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:27.640
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.600
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.160
I'm Sean. I'm Mockrider.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.100
And I'm Sam.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.540
That's not true. You are not Mockrider.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.500
I am Mockrider.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.240
Everybody's Mockrider. I thought I was Mockrider.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.600
No, no, no. I thought it would be nice to have a little callback to this episode.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.320
Well, it's the 200th episode.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.500
Very exciting.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:50.940
Lots to celebrate. And I'm sure, like, for all of our listeners, it's also quite a celebration for everybody

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.620
who's been listening for 200 straight episodes.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.900
You know, we can go around the table real quick and just talk about our thoughts on that.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.380
Sean, let's start with you.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.620
200 episodes.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.840
Is it a big deal?

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.540
I mean, like, it's pretty much four years.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:07.900
That's a lot.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.400
I didn't really expect this to go on for four years, if I'm being honest.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.240
But I think, yeah, that's a big deal.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:18.320
It's a lot.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.920
Joe, did you think we'd make it?

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.420
I thought we'd make it.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.660
But, you know, I was never sure.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.500
I was never sure. When we started, I was like, you know, maybe we'll lose interest.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.860
But no, I think our interest, if anything, has just ramped up as we go.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.600
So, you know, I'm excited to have gotten this far and to keep going.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.960
Sam, did you ever think you'd come back?

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.980
Of course. I knew I'd be back.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.840
Yeah. I'm like, I've mentioned this to someone on Twitter.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.260
But I've just been kind of focusing on music, which kind of sounds like pretentious.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.220
Like, I'm really just focusing on my music right now.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.320
But like, it's not.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.580
I'm in a band. We practice a lot.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.120
It's like, it takes up a lot of time.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.740
Because you have to like practice before the practice too, which is, you know, a lot of time.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.380
Homework practice. Yeah, it's homework practice and regular practice.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.660
So, I don't know.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:16.920
I will be back.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:23.260
I can't say in what capacity, but this is something that is dear to me.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.540
And I want to continue to be a part of it.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.440
And, you know, however I can.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:33.660
Sometimes I do like a homework practice for just speaking about things.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.980
It doesn't really, it doesn't really pay off that well because I still fumble over words a lot.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.760
But when I'm not recording the show, I'm usually trying to exclaim opinions on things.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:56.800
And, of course, this being the 200th episode means that it has been a little more than three years of nonstop recording of nostalgia episodes.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.000
We started with 10-yard fight back, the published date was February 9th, 2018.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.400
But we recorded it actually in early January.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.940
It was one of my New Year's resolutions for 2018 to start a podcast.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.700
And all four of us made an appearance on that.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:21.120
We did 10-yard fight, baseball, Clu Clu Land, and Duck Hunt all in the same record.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.040
Guys, what were we smoking back then?

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.380
Wait a minute. No, we did Duck, we did out of order.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:26.720
We did Excitebike.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.180
And then we went back and did Duck Hunt for some reason because we didn't have the zappers yet.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.320
Yo, that is great trivia.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.920
You are the master of the trivia over here.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:37.160
Wait, we did Excitebike first?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:38.900
Yeah, Excitebike was day one.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.380
It was some real demo tape shit. Like, we had one mic talking into it from across a room.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.260
Sounds terrible.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:45.960
Go back and listen.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.960
Yeah, you can definitely tell.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.960
It's so bad that I actually put a disclaimer in front of those episodes.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.940
I think those first four episodes probably Excitebike instead of Duck Hunt saying like,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:02.380
hey, you should just know that like a lot of people think that this is an okay podcast

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.500
and this is not okay, but you should listen to these episodes,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.300
but also listen to the newer episodes because they're a little higher fidelity.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:16.140
Of course, you know, I just want to take one moment to thank our audio producer, Chris,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.220
who has been with us on the whole ride too.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.160
He doesn't speak, but you might know him as an occasional voice actor on the show.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:25.260
He plays a couple different roles.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.120
I won't say what they were because that would spoil the magic,

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.140
but we've had some fun moments with Chris as well,

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:32.180
and we couldn't do this without him on the show.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.980
So I just wanted to give him a quick shout out as well,

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.500
even though he insists still on not speaking.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.380
Come on, Chris.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:38.560
He has a mic.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.500
He could speak right now if he wanted, but...

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:47.200
We do have a very special announcement though for us and for fans of Nostalgia.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:52.540
We've been paying close attention for the last probably like three years,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.380
you know, since the beginning of the podcast really.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.140
We've been asking the listeners, what do you guys think of the show?

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.120
What matters most to you guys?

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:07.120
What was surprising was is that the most requested feature out of anything for Nostalgia

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.600
was something that was kind of always there in the beginning

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:13.380
and was one of the main like concepts of the show.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.240
But then when we lost, you know, like the ability to just continue to fund it,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.720
like we were losing money on it, we shut it down.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:21.960
And that was the website.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.100
The website is something where people constantly tweeted us being like,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:26.420
Hey, what happened to this?

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.940
Or why isn't this working?

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.100
And we were trying to explain to them like, you know,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.880
it's just something that's just not profitable for us to run.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.740
But we know that's what a lot of you have been asking for is, you know,

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.120
when can the website come back?

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.800
And how can I give money so that the website could come back?

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.760
And, you know, we were really appreciative in general,

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:47.160
just the sentiment too, that like the website was something that you guys wanted to see.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.040
But we couldn't just ask for money for a website.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.760
That's not like, that's not who we are.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.560
That's not what we set out to make the show was the show has never been profitable.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:01.320
It's an expensive show to run, but we love doing it week in and week out.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.780
And that's why, you know, we're looking at, well, how can we have the website?

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.380
How can we make something new for listeners?

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:15.400
And how can we make sure that this show, Nostalgia, the show that you've been listening to for 200 plus episodes,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.300
continues to be the same exact thing without compromise?

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:21.320
So we're bringing on advertisements.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:25.020
And that's not true. We've actually done some advertisements, but they're gone.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.440
Yeah. They're gone.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.760
If you go back to older episodes, they're not there.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.220
But new episodes sometimes have answered, but then like those are also designed to be removed.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:36.540
It's pretty smart. Trust me.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.860
It's like one of those things where we always record the episode without the ads this way.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:41.080
They can be removed later.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:49.300
So what we decided on doing is, you know, nothing about Nostalgia, this show, the show that you subscribe to every Friday,

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:51.780
nothing about that is changing at all.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:07:01.860
You will still be able to get this episode and episode 201, 202, 203 every Friday without compromise guaranteed to you.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:13.660
The new thing that we are offering on a special thing that we have in the show notes, a link to our Patreon page is for just $1 a month.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.140
That's all we're really asking for is $1 a month.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:23.660
You are helping fund, you know, the, the, what we need to have this website up and running.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.180
We do it through Squarespace.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:33.560
So it's not too much per year or anything like that, but just the costs of us maintaining the website and using Squarespace and all that.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:37.160
We're going to make a special thank you page to everybody.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:41.640
And you can, you know, let us know obviously what you want to be titled as in those things.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:42.940
You don't have to use your real name.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:55.500
But this way, you know, everybody knows who's helping get this website back up and running because, you know, whether you're someone who's going to contribute or not to this Patreon, that is something that a lot of people are asking for.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.600
And so we think we should give everybody the chance to make this a feature.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:07.520
That said, we're not going to wait for people to, you know, to hit a certain amount of subscribers before we launch up the website.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.420
We're comfortable taking that cost on.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:17.420
And, you know, the website will come back and we're happy to say that all the information that you need from Nostalgia and to catch up will be there very soon.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.060
Web 3.0, baby.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.320
Yeah, I can't promise it'll be up when this episode's up, but it will be up very soon.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:42.590
In addition to that, though, for people who are big fans of Nostalgia and want more of the podcast, more than what we offer here as a chronological exploration, we are offering for $5 a month access to a new show called Nostalgia Bites.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.300
That's B-Y-T-E-S.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.880
Get it? These are bite-sized episodes delivered once a month.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:52.060
Like it's bite-sized.

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:55.100
So like a bite out of food.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.640
Right, right. A bite out of food, but it's a computer bite.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.720
Yeah. B-Y-T-E.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.620
Yeah. These shorter episodes are once a month.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:12.500
They are exclusive looks for these $5 a month Patreon subscribers who basically it's going to take a look at the Famicom library.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:19.500
And we're going to look at the games that you can play without any Japanese language requirements.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.840
You don't need to know any Japanese.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:26.700
You can, as long as you have access to these Famicom games, you can play them just like we're going to play them.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:27.940
And we're going to look at them.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.700
Again, it's only once a month, so it's not every Friday or anything like that.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:39.900
But we're hoping to not just look at all of the Famicom games and say like, oh, this is, yeah, this is another one.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.360
And it's the same thing that we played kind of as this other episode, but it's this.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:49.520
No, this is going to be looking at some of the best and unique Famicom games.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:58.320
And, you know, I hope, I guess like, you know, the hope is that everybody will also play along and experience these for the first time.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:04.400
Because the hook of nostalgia really is you guys have all played these games or are familiar with these games.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:12.460
And you're hearing a younger generation's take on what these games, you know, are like today with the modern lens.

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:22.160
But with Nostalgia Bites, I'd argue that there's probably not too many of you guys that have delved into the, have dived in to the Famicom catalog.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:29.660
And that's what we're really hoping to have access to here with Nostalgia Bites is we can all experience something new together for the very first time.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.520
Again, it's just $5 a month to get access to that.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:35.520
You'll get a special RSS feed.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:41.300
And you'll still get, obviously, the thank you on the Nostalgia website and all that.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.580
But there's definitely going to be more bonuses along the way for everyone, though.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.180
We're looking into other ideas.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.160
You know, we, I don't want to promise anything.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.880
But we're always thinking about other types of shows we can do or other types of bonus content we can give you guys.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:00.780
So while we don't want to promise that directly, Nostalgia Bites is something we can promise.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:07.420
And we're really looking forward to that, that first episode, which, you know, for more information, definitely check out our Twitter.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.020
But we'll also be talking about it here on the podcast as well.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.900
Guys, anything you want to say about this new Nostalgia Bites?

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.800
Any excitement? Anything you want to tease or anything like that?

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:17.860
I am excited.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:26.060
I really look forward to us having a home on the worldwide internet superhighway again.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:38.140
And I can't wait to get into the Famicom stuff and brainstorming about what else we can throw onto that old Patrioni.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:46.020
Yeah, it's exciting to try these new games now from a different perspective.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:51.800
We're getting these games that might be totally different than anything we've played and little extra bonuses.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:55.780
And yeah, likewise, whatever we can come up with down the road, too.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.260
You know, some other things that we can do, other types of episodes.

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:00.920
It's really fun to be able to branch out.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:07.760
So I'm just excited for the website so I can finally figure out, is NES baseball haunted?

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:14.300
Which is our most requested article because I think strictly because we reference it so much on the show.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:18.880
But yes, soon everybody will know if NES baseball is or is not haunted.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.380
We don't take a firm stance on it.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:26.880
But it's up for all true believers to understand and to make their own final assumptions for.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:35.480
I just – one thing, if you don't mind me sounding like a broken record here, I just want everyone to know that we sincerely appreciate how much the show has grown.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:45.480
And that's why we made the decision of whether you're a $1 a month subscriber, a $5 a month subscriber for the bonus podcast, or you don't subscribe at all.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.960
We're not removing anybody's access to nostalgia, this podcast.

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:55.840
It's very important to us that everybody who's been on the ride stays on the ride.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:00.880
We couldn't have made it to 200 episodes without the growth we've been seeing.

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:12.660
So even if you can't pledge any money to whether the funding of the website or the new podcast, we want you guys to know that the best way you can support us is just through word of mouth.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:20.660
Telling your friends, hey, I've been listening to this show for X amount of months or years I've been listening to this show, and I think you should too.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:24.040
That is the number one thing that this podcast needs is just growth.

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:37.260
And we've been diving into our own pockets a lot throughout the show's run to do advertising or to run stuff on Reddit or do the Game Jam.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:41.340
All those things are with our own time and effort and money and stuff like that.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.740
So we really appreciate just everybody who comes in and listens every week because it is a consistent growth.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:51.600
We've only ever seen the numbers go up, and we really appreciate everybody who's doing this.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:58.780
And if I could just jump in real quick, I will say that one of my favorite things to do now is to sell out.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:02.540
So this is really playing into my strengths.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:04.980
I can confirm that, yeah.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:07.840
Sean sells out a lot.

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.000
Anyway, we do have an episode to talk about.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:25.920
This is not just some kind of celebration thing.

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.020
We won't be reviewing the Essential Games list.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:31.000
We won't magically put Zelda II on the list today.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:36.500
Never going to happen. We have a real schedule of things to do, and that is mostly to talk about Strider.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:45.100
The Capcom, I think to some people this is a classic, but I found that to be a little confusing in terms of information

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:49.540
because they developed an arcade game and this NES game at the same time.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.040
So I'm not sure which one was actually the revered one.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:58.180
It feels like my research led me to believe that the arcade one is the one that's more famous in terms of longevity.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:07.460
But this NES game was well-received at the time, and it's kind of like an early mix of the games that we've been getting,

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.520
like Ninja Gaiden meets a Metroid-style game.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:12.120
Would you agree with that assessment, guys?

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.960
Yeah. I've heard someone compare it to Bionic Commando also.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:19.540
Oh, that's a good comparison, yeah.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:21.900
Yeah, I can definitely see that.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:27.680
It's like a lot of these games, I mean, even back to like, so there's some elements of like Metal Gear in here

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.720
as like you're finding clues and you got your home base that you communicate with and stuff.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:38.800
I think it's like, it feels like it's on that same path as games like that that we've had intermittently.

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:51.420
Yeah, like a strong, it leans on some sort of story that actually comes out through the gameplay a bit more than your standard NES game.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:58.360
It's got some cinematic elements, so I can see that like Metal Gear comparison there.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:07.100
So in this game, you play as, and I'm probably going to butcher this, but I think it's Hear You, or Her You for all I know,

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:13.140
but you take control of the main character who is a, he's the youngest ever in the elite class known as Strider.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:21.160
They're like a secret organization that probably is responsible for a lot of the, you know, the Illuminati stuff of the world.

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:29.620
And in this side-scrolling platform game, you play as him and have to locate clues, which are kind of like floppy disks,

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.660
and interrogate members of either rival factions or of your own faction.

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:40.760
And these clues are required in order to continue progression in the story and unlock other levels,

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:44.460
but also move further along in previous levels too.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:50.480
There's some degree of backtracking, moving forward, moving the story along,

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:57.600
and power-up system that requires you to kind of like go back and try your new power-ups in places that were already familiar to you.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:03.920
Not much unlike how it was in Metroid where you would acquire, say, you know, missiles,

00:17:04.100 --> 00:17:07.540
and now you can open up doors specifically with missiles and stuff like that.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:11.740
I feel like, you know, on paper, it's a pretty solid idea.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.940
Not that it's anything too original, right?

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.000
We've seen this all before.

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:19.620
But the one that stood out to me is the idea that you have to collect clues,

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.940
almost like, you know, a detective, you know, get the next beat in the story,

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:29.100
in order to then go back to your main hub, analyze the files,

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:35.160
and get an idea of where you need to either go back to or where you need to go next.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:36.680
Sean, any thoughts on that?

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.920
Yeah, I really like that element of it.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:42.780
The files aren't...

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:45.480
There's a lot of games that do something like this,

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:53.780
where there are the things that are revealed to you in the game are how you have to progress.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:18:01.260
I guess like it's sort of been done in, like, Castlevania II and semi-adventure games like that,

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:05.300
where, oh, you have to learn this information from some NPC.

00:18:05.740 --> 00:18:07.860
But at least in this one, it's a lot more clear.

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.600
And that there's a whole system for it.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:17.480
Like, you go back to your base, and you use your computer to analyze,

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:25.580
and it's not super on the nose, but it gives you an idea of where you have to go.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:27.000
I really like that.

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:28.580
It's sort of like you're piecing it together.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:35.740
And I just like the idea of a home base in general to sort of tie the game together.

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.540
So, yeah, I was a big fan of that.

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:45.020
Yeah, and it makes that home base like sort of the catalyst to, I guess,

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:47.920
what would be sort of like Metroidvania-y,

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.600
where you have to go back to the home base and then like go somewhere you've already been,

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.140
but now with the new tools you have.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:53.980
Yeah, it's like a central room.

00:18:54.680 --> 00:18:57.220
Yeah. But I was going to ask you guys, would you,

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:03.180
because I feel like this game has like elements that it acts like it's got these RPG elements.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:07.220
But I can't decide if I would call it more of an RPG or more of like Metroidvania.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.660
Because it gives you... I'd say it's more an adventure game.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:13.580
Well, right. But I mean in those elements that you're like,

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:17.320
in the elements that give you that progression, you know,

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:20.240
that like, oh, I can't get past this wall until I get this item.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:26.900
I think the difference here is that like an RPG would be system-based in terms of how you progress.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:31.720
Whereas in this, like there are specific moments where you level up.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.860
It's like when you talk to a specific character and you can't,

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:37.720
I don't think there's any sequence breaking.

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:41.440
Like you, we will always level up when you talk to these characters.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.220
Right. And that's what I thought too, for the most part.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:45.760
But I just thought it was interesting that they like,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:49.280
we sort of talked about this recently in the boat game,

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:50.900
which we just played and I can't remember the name of it.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:52.400
The Cove triangle.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:55.300
But we sort of talked about this recently,

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:56.280
the idea that like,

00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:57.960
it's giving you like this,

00:19:58.060 --> 00:19:59.820
it's almost by calling them level ups

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.320
and letting your health and your power move up every time.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.200
I almost felt like it was giving me the illusion of like,

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:06.560
oh, it's an RPG and I'm growing.

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:09.440
But really it's just, I've passed this part of the game,

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:11.260
so I've unlocked the ability to do this.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:14.360
Like, it's very linear in that sense.

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:15.280
Not that that's a bad thing.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:16.220
I just thought it was interesting that it,

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.100
I don't think we've ever seen that kind of thing presented as like levels.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:22.180
Yeah. Because levels usually associate it with like my own skill,

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:25.080
you know, rather than just like how far I got in the game.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:29.380
Yeah, I agree. It is kind of misleading,

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:32.760
but I guess it's not really hurting anything.

00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:34.780
No, not at all. I just thought it was interesting.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:36.840
Yeah. Joe, if I could pose the question to you,

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:38.680
would you have preferred,

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:42.060
you know, we've seen in games like Rambo,

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:43.500
I don't know why that comes to my head,

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:44.340
but, you know,

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:47.600
where killing enemies gains you XP,

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:48.940
even in an adventure,

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.440
action adventure style game.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.920
We've seen that version of that.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:55.480
Did you prefer something like that?

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:56.780
Would you have preferred that like,

00:20:57.140 --> 00:20:58.420
as in Strider,

00:20:58.580 --> 00:20:59.980
like for enemies you kill,

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.380
you then level up experience to gain like more points in your health bar,

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:06.700
or learn new tricks?

00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:08.840
Or was it better to say like,

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.200
no, you have to complete this kind,

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:14.460
you know, like the next step in the mission based on the files you analyzed,

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.220
and then you get this like fake level up.

00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:19.920
So by the, based on the way that this game is designed,

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:21.440
I think that the way it is,

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:23.580
is, is the correct choice for them.

00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:26.200
I agree. Because it, because it locks you behind these things and,

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:29.920
and, and lets you kind of like experience this adventure in like the,

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:31.700
the optimal order that they want you to,

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:32.780
where you're learning things as,

00:21:32.820 --> 00:21:33.720
as you need to and stuff.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.520
Um, so I wasn't trying to argue that it should be that way,

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:37.420
but I think like,

00:21:37.900 --> 00:21:41.300
you know, if they, if we wanted to like entertain that thought for a second,

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.580
I think that there's room for it to be like the items you get,

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:45.540
you get from the,

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:46.900
from the enemies and the levels,

00:21:46.900 --> 00:21:49.020
as far as your health and your HP and your,

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.720
what do you really call it? The mana or whatever,

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:54.780
your energy bar that levels up based on your experience and the other stuff

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.460
that you need to progress levels up based on where you are in the game.

00:21:57.620 --> 00:21:58.600
I think there's room for that.

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:00.140
I don't think it's, it's necessary,

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:05.220
but yeah, I think this would have to be some kind of immersive sim for it to,

00:22:05.300 --> 00:22:08.540
to actually work like a system based level up system.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:10.660
I think that with how,

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:15.980
uh, since you don't really have that much latitude in how you complete this game,

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:17.700
the way they have it now is fine.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:18.820
But you know, Joe,

00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:20.120
you did remind me,

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:21.820
uh, and it is a different style game.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:23.280
So Sean, your point's not wrong here.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:24.880
Uh, you reminded me of Rygar,

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:26.120
which, uh, you know,

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:27.300
has a,

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:30.280
uh, you know, kill a lot of enemies to get,

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:31.960
uh, stronger and,

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:33.580
you know, gain more HP,

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:36.920
but you don't necessarily like gain a new,

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:38.920
like breaking items or,

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.280
you know, like that would destroy the,

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:42.480
the level based design that they had set up for you.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.260
Uh, that could have been interesting for Strider if it was,

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:49.740
uh, if that's just how your energy and health were earned was through like,

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:50.900
you know, experience points,

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:53.600
but then the, the items you acquire are,

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:54.580
are level based.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:55.680
Yeah. You know,

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:56.800
it feels almost like it's like,

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.980
okay, that sounds like more of just like this Christmas list wish of like,

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:00.900
Oh, that'd be cool to have.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:03.860
But like, in no way is that a complaint about what we do have here.

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:10.420
Yeah. They'd have to rework a lot of stuff because this game also has that fun NES thing,

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:14.340
uh, where enemies just spawn and spawn and spawn.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:16.620
So you could just stand in front of an,

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.440
an enemy conveyor belt and,

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:21.520
and just keep like spamming attack and then you're max level.

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:23.600
That's true. I hadn't thought of that.

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.020
Is the collecting of the floppy disks,

00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:39.320
a unique idea,

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:41.420
or is that just the same as,

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:47.520
you know, we've seen in other games collect X number of things and you'll be able to move on from that particular level.

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.180
Uh, you know,

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:50.540
I'm kind of drawing some blanks here,

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:51.680
but you know,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:53.520
even early, yeah,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:54.380
go ahead, John. Well,

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:56.600
it may not be a unique idea.

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:59.880
It, the context of it is at least,

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:00.920
unique where,

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:07.420
it's, I guess it's based in reality enough that I kind of buy it as like,

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:11.140
Oh, I need to find this so I can figure out where to go next instead of the game.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:13.860
Just only literally letting me go to where I need to go.

00:24:14.360 --> 00:24:16.500
Um, and it's,

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.040
it's a little bit less contrived,

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.960
uh, than a collect X amount of X,

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:21.940
X amount of Y.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:26.900
Um, because in the fiction of the game,

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:29.840
like make finding information via,

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:31.100
floppy disks makes sense.

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:33.620
So I, I at least liked the,

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:37.280
uh, I at least liked the context of it,

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:39.180
if not the mechanic itself.

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:42.180
And I also liked the idea of like,

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:45.200
you're looking through the levels for these things.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:47.340
And it's not that like you're getting to the end,

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:49.320
like the way that the game is constructed,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.400
where you have to like return to levels and go back and search.

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:53.920
It kind of,

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:56.820
it makes sense that you're like searching for like,

00:24:56.820 --> 00:24:59.760
like Sean was saying, it makes sense in like the scope of the story.

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.000
And also in terms of like how the levels are,

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:05.440
like if you were just getting to the end of each level,

00:25:06.180 --> 00:25:07.200
if it was like a linear,

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.080
a linear thing.

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:09.980
And then at the end of each level,

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:11.900
there's like data for you.

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:13.160
And it's like, Oh, okay. Like,

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:15.240
this is the information I need to get to the next level.

00:25:15.360 --> 00:25:16.740
And then it just sends you to the next one.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.860
Like that wouldn't be as engaging.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:22.940
I don't think, but I think that the way that the levels are constructed makes sense with this

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:24.800
narrative as well. Yeah.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.940
And that fact that you're searching also coupled with the fact that the things

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:29.980
that you, that you find don't always,

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:33.000
I mean, sometimes they do, but they don't always just unlock a new place.

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:35.140
Like sometimes they just give you information,

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.860
like actual real life information that you have to apply,

00:25:38.060 --> 00:25:41.280
you know, out here in your own brain before you can figure out where to go.

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:43.060
It's not like it just says you got this,

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:44.960
your character knows what's going on.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:46.500
So now the new place is unlocked.

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:47.540
It's like, you got this.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:48.660
I'm like, Oh, maybe I got to go,

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:49.660
you know, go back to,

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.480
to Australia where I already was,

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:52.420
or a bad example.

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:53.400
Cause that's the last place you go,

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:56.920
but you know, like somewhere that you've already been and like reminds you of something or

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:58.780
tells you, it gives you real context,

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:03.060
which makes it feel like a little more like you're in charge rather than the

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:03.960
game is just guiding you.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:06.200
Yeah. And so like setup wise,

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.100
it's great, right?

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:10.800
The idea of collecting these floppy disks and then going to your home base,

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:12.140
analyzing the information,

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.480
receiving, the Intel and being like,

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.020
okay, now either,

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:20.160
you know, like a new place like Egypt might've opened up or actually have to go back to

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:20.980
a previous level.

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:22.600
And, uh, you know,

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:23.500
with my new power ups,

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:26.980
try an area that I saw before that I wasn't quite sure what was going on

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.040
there, but maybe now my new power ups,

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:30.080
or as they're referred to in this game,

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.100
tricks will,

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:32.240
will do something for me.

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.260
But before we get to like,

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:35.700
you know, exploration and all that,

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:42.540
it's worth mentioning that the actual execution presentationally of these things isn't as

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:43.820
great as we're making it sound.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:45.120
And I'm not saying it's terrible,

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:46.800
but it does have its flaws.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:48.000
Uh, number one,

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:50.400
just being the presentation of the floppy disks.

00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:52.720
I found that to be very strange how they don't,

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:55.200
they're not like immediately present in the room.

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.780
Sometimes they'll just drop down from the sky.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:26:59.980
Uh, or like people will,

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:02.800
you know, even with your power boots and stuff like that,

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:03.740
like they're not there,

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:06.460
but then when you slide into the area or,

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:08.160
you know, walk enough into the area,

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:09.660
the boots just happened to,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.180
to spawn from thin air.

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:13.480
I don't know if they were trying to like present it as like,

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:14.740
Oh, you know, if you saw them,

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:16.000
then you would know to go there.

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:18.280
You have, we have to encourage exploration,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:19.820
but I didn't like how,

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:22.720
how they just spawn out of thin air in those places,

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:23.940
especially with, um,

00:27:24.500 --> 00:27:26.180
the characters that give you information.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.380
Uh, you know, if you run into like some of the scientists,

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:29.400
or stuff like that,

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:32.800
they, they trigger like a cut scene of just text,

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.600
but then after that,

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:39.360
then still a floppy disk or a power up will just randomly spawn.

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:43.300
Then you've collected that power up or say it's the,

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:44.460
the floppy disk.

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:46.680
Now you are stuck with,

00:27:46.940 --> 00:27:51.060
uh, the only possible way to go back to your ship is backtracking through

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:52.780
something that you've already done.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:54.340
Uh, which is,

00:27:54.440 --> 00:27:55.460
which is only tedious,

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:58.400
right? You haven't acquired a new power up that now makes going back

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:00.300
backwards easier or anything like that.

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:01.880
You just have your floppy disk.

00:28:02.020 --> 00:28:04.020
You need to find out what the information is on it.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.820
And you have to do the whole level again,

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:08.220
backwards. And by, when I say whole level,

00:28:08.220 --> 00:28:09.760
it's not too long of an experience,

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:11.480
probably like five minutes or so,

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:15.240
but you have to do everything you just did once more in the opposite

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:19.660
direction. And it doesn't encourage like taking a different path or a different,

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:21.360
uh, series of tubes.

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:22.600
Uh, I think actually sometimes,

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:23.700
sometimes it does.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:24.960
Yeah. Like,

00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:26.260
you know, it's very,

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:29.240
um, it's really inconsistent.

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:34.840
Cause there are some instances where it will let you walk out the,

00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:37.680
like the most recent door that you walked in.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:39.620
And then you're just back at your base.

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:43.560
And then there are the ones that you go into that series of tubes.

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:49.600
Um, and it conveniently lets you out very near the beginning where you can get

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:50.460
back to base.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:51.520
